L113: **Effects of endurance and endurance–strength training on endothelial function in women with obesity: A randomized trial**
L114: **SUPPLEMENTARY MATERIAL**
L115: Figure S1. Training volume diagram of each session for both interventions
L116: The 3-month intervention consisted of physical
L117: exercise program including 3 sessions of training per
L118: week, with a total of 36 training sessions for each group.
L119: The training programs of both groups were comparable in
L120: exercise volume, and varied only in the nature of the effort
L121: (Figure S1).
L122: Endurance training consisted of a 5-min low-intensity
L123: warm-up (stretching exercises; 50–60% of maximum heart
L124: rate (HR), 45 min of training at an intensity of 60–80% of
L125: maximum HR, 5 min of non–weight-bearing cycling,
L126: finishing with 5 min of low-intensity warm-down
L127: stretching and breathing exercises (Figure S2).
L128: Combined endurance–strength training consisted of a
L129: 5-min low-intensity warm-up (stretching exercises; 50–
L130: 60% of maximum HR), 20 min of strength exercises using a
L131: barbell and gymnastics ball, 25 min of endurance exercise
L132: using cycle ergometers at an intensity of 60–80% of
L133: maximum HR, 5 min of non–weight-bearing cycling, and 5
L134: min of low-intensity cool down stretching and breathing
L135: exercises (Figure S3). Due to the need for regeneration of
L136: muscle power, the strength component was variable and
L137: repeated each week. On Mondays, upper limb exercises
L138: were performed with a neck barbell; Wednesdays
L139: involved spine-stabilizing exercises, deep muscle-forming
L140: exercises, and balance-adjusting exercises with a
L141: gymnastic ball; on Fridays, lower limb exercises with a
L142: barbell were carried out (Table S1). The number of
L143: repetitions in the sets was dependent on the subjects’
L144: capabilities and was equal to the number of repetitions
L145: performed correctly ≈ 50–80% of their one-repetition
L146: maximum. The number of repetitions was systematically
L147: increased with the increase in subject’s muscle strength.
L148: HR during both physical trainings was monitored with a
L149: Suunto Fitness Solution® device (Suunto, Vantaa,
L150: Finland).
